// `float` is a part of the type beside `int`, never inside it (ticket 69), and
// nothing flows between the two (ticket 80): `Mean([]) -> 0` under this
// signature is refused, and so is `List.Sum(xs) / List.Length(xs)`, which
// ticket 38 made truncating division over two ints. The conversion is written
// where the author wants it, `Float.FromInt(n)` (ticket 81), and `/` between
// two floats is the BEAM's own division.
module Stats

public float Mean(list<int> samples)

Mean([]) -> 0.0
Mean(xs) -> Float.FromInt(List.Sum(xs)) / Float.FromInt(List.Length(xs))

// A float literal in a head is one value under `=:=`: `0.0` and not `0`, and
// on OTP 27+ not `-0.0` either. The rest of the part has no head to paste, as
// `atom \ :ok` has none, so the second clause is the catch-all a float
// dispatch always ends with.
public atom Verdict(float mean)

Verdict(0.0) -> :empty
Verdict(_)   -> :some

// The value that reaches `Verdict` is the float `Mean` wrote, never an `int`
// the compiler converted, so `Check([])` is `:empty`.
public atom Check(list<int> xs)

Check(xs) -> Verdict(Mean(xs))

// A float guard compares as the BEAM compares and credits nothing to
// exhaustiveness: the part carries no intervals, so a clause set over
// `float` closes with a catch-all, as one over `atom` does.
public atom Sign(float x)

Sign(x) when x < 0.0 -> :negative
Sign(_)              -> :nonnegative
